{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Here is a 300+ word summary of the Wikipedia page \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raymond_III,_Count_of_Tripoli\" without commas and with at least 3 highlighted sections in markdown format:\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Antioch and ruled the County of Tripoli from 1148 to 1183\nHe was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Helena of Antioch\nRaymond III succeeded his father at the age of six after Raymond II was murdered in 1148\nHe was placed under the regency of Joscelin II Count of Edessa until he came of age\n\n*Raymond III played a crucial role in the Second Crusade although he opposed the Siege of Damascus in 1148*\n\nRaymond III came of age in 1156 and took control of the County of Tripoli\nHe married Agnes of Châtillon in 1159 but the marriage was annulled in 1163\nRaymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Inab\nHe was released in 1165 after paying a large ransom\n\n*The County of Tripoli under Raymond III experienced a period of relative peace and prosperity despite being surrounded by Muslim states*\n\nRaymond III married Maria of Antioch in 1169\nHe played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and supported the claim of Guy of Lusignan to the throne\nRaymond III was appointed regent for the young Humphrey IV Lord of Toron in 1176\n\n*Raymond III death in 1183 marked the end of the Antiochene dynasty in the County of Tripoli and led to a succession crisis*\n\nRaymond III was succeeded by William I Sanseverino who was appointed by King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem\nThe County of Tripoli continued to exist until it was conquered by the Mamluk Sultanate in 1289\nRaymond III legacy is still debated among historians with some viewing him as a capable ruler and others as indecisive \n\nNote: The highlighted sections are \n1 *Raymond III played a crucial role